.TH CBMAP_GET_ELEM_COPY 3 "2026-09-13" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cbmap_get_elem_copy \- copy the value associated with a key out of a BST map
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

ccol_retval_t cbmap_get_elem_copy(cbmap cbm, const cmap_pair *key_pair,
                                  void *target_buf, size_t target_buf_size);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Searches the map for key_pair and copies the value of that key into
target_buf. The size of the buffer must match the size of the stored
value exactly. The function never does a partial copy, so it never
truncates a value without a report. This rule is stricter than the rule
of chmap_get_elem_copy(3), which copies min(value_size,
target_buf_size) bytes instead.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cbm              BST map to search
key_pair         Key to look up
target_buf       Buffer that receives the copy of the value
target_buf_size  Size of target_buf. It must equal the size of the
                 stored value
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success        - The function found the key and copied the value
                      into target_buf
ccol_key_not_found  - The key is not in the map
ccol_invalid_args   - key_pair is NULL, key_pair carries a NULL ptr
                      with a non-zero size, target_buf is NULL
                      with a non-zero target_buf_size,
                      target_buf_size does not
                      match the size of the stored value, or the size
                      of key_pair does not match the size of a
                      fixed-width key type (see cbmap_create_full(3))
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The ptr of a key_pair needs no particular alignment. The comparison
reads the key bytes with memcpy and never through a pointer cast to the
key type. A caller can therefore point key_pair->ptr straight at key
bytes that sit at an arbitrary offset inside a larger buffer, such as a
field sliced out of a packed network frame or a memory-mapped record,
without copying them into an aligned local first.
O(log n) complexity
The two value sizes must match exactly. chmap_get_elem_copy(3) does not
need an exact match
The function does not touch target_buf on any failure
The function asserts if cbm is NULL
The function reports a NULL key_pair as ccol_invalid_args. This matches
chmap_get_elem_copy(3)
A key_pair whose ptr is NULL and whose size is not zero promises bytes
that it has no storage for. The function reports it as
ccol_invalid_args too
A key_pair of size zero is correct, with a ptr or without one. It looks
up the empty key
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cbmap_get_elem_ref (3),
.BR cbmap_insert_elem (3),
.BR cbmap_create_full (3)
